$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Preparation of 3(S)-4-acetyl-1-benzyl-3-phenylpiperazin-2-one (6n). To a
0 °C stirring suspension of L-phenylglycine methyl ester hydrochloride
(154 mg, 0.764 mmol, 1.0 equiv.), 4Å powdered molecular sieves (250 mg),
acetic acid (130 μL, 2.29 mmol, 3.0 equiv.) and sodium
triacetoxyborohydride (243 mg, 1.15 mmol, 1.5 equiv.) in 5 mL
acetonitrile was added dropwise over 5 minutes a 2.5 mL acetonitrile
solution of aldehyde 4a (161 mg, 0.840 mmol, 1.1 equiv.). After stirring
for 90 minutes, the ice bath was removed and the reaction was warmed to
ambient temperature and stirred for 18 hours. The reaction was heated to
reflux for 16 hours, cooled and poured into aqueous saturated NaHCO3,
which was extracted three times with dichloromethane. The combined
organic extracts were dried (Na2SO4), filtered and concentrated in vacuo.
Purification by flash chromatography (2% methanol in dichloromethane)
provided piperazinone 6n as a clear oil (198 mg, 84% yield). HRMS (ES)
exact mass calculated for C19H21N2O2 (M+H+): 309.1599; found 309.1601.
Chiral HPLC analysis (Chiralcel OD column 250 X 4.6 mm; 220 nm; flow =
1.0 mL/minute of A/B:90/10 (A=hexanes, B=2-propanol); 20 minute run. 84%
enantiomeric excess. 1H NMR (500 MHz, CDCl3) (Rotamer A:B; 2:1) Rotamer
A: δ 7.45 (br d, J = 7.6 Hz, 1H), 7.41 (br t, J = 7.2 Hz, 2H), 7.38-7.26
(m, 6H), 7.18 (br d, J = 6.6 Hz, 1H), 5.57 (s, 1H), 4.72 (d, J = 14.6 Hz,
1H), 4.54 (d, J = 14.6 Hz, 1H), 4.14 (br dt, J = 13.4, 6.0 Hz, 1H),
3.50-3.43 (m, 1H), 3.33-3.23 (m, 1H), 3.23-3.17 (m, 1H), 2.11 (s, 3H);
Rotamer B: δ 7.45 (br d, J = 7.6 Hz, 1H), 7.41 (br t, J = 7.2 Hz, 1H),
7.38-7.26 (m, 7H), 7.18 (br d, J = 6.6 Hz, 1H), 6.38 (s, 1H), 4.83 (d, J
= 14.6 Hz, 1H), 4.57 (d, J = 14.6 Hz, 1H), 3.72-3.65 (m, 1H), 3.56 (ddd,
J = 13.4, 9.8, 4.0 Hz, 1H), 3.37 (ddd, J = 16.4, 12.7, 4.3 Hz, 1H),
3.33-3.23 (m, 1H), 2.16 (s, 3H).Retention time for 6n = 12.9 minutes
(Area % = 74.0), enantiomer retention time = 16.4 minutes (Area % =
10.6);
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM mol. sieves used
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Preparation of Substituted Piperazinones via Tandem Reductive
Amination-(N,N'-Acyl Transfer)-Cyclization
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Beshore, Douglas C.; Dinsmore, Christopher J.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Organic Letters (2002), 4(7), 1201-1204

